\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N=\{1,2,\ldots\}$, and $\mathbb Z,\mathbb Q,\mathbb R,\mathbb C$ denote the integers, rationals, reals and complex numbers. Any other conventions are specified locally.


An affine line through two distinct points $x,y\in\mathbb R^2$ is denoted by $\ell(x,y)$. For a finite set $A\subset\mathbb R^2$, let
\[
 T(A)=\bigl|\{(x,y)\in A^2:x\ne y,\ (\ell(x,y)\cap A)\setminus\{x,y\}\ne\varnothing\}\bigr|.
\]
Pairs are ordered. A cubic curve means the zero set of a nonzero real polynomial in two variables of total degree at most three; it may be reducible.
\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
For every integer $k\geq2$ and every real $\delta>0$, does there exist $c=c(k,\delta)>0$ such that, for every finite set $A\subset\mathbb R^2$ of cardinality $n\geq1$ with at most $k$ points on any line and $T(A)\geq\delta n^2$, there is a nonzero $F\in\mathbb R[X,Y]$ of total degree at most three with
\[
 |\{a\in A:F(a)=0\}|\geq cn?
\] \href{https://people.maths.ox.ac.uk/greenbj/papers/open-problems.pdf}{[S2]}

\subsection{Short English statement}
Must a planar point set with bounded line multiplicities and many pairs lying in collinear triples have a positive proportion of its points on one cubic curve?

\subsection{Sources}
\begin{description}
\item[S1] ULAM.AI and the UnsolvedMath Contributors, \emph{UnsolvedMath: A Curated Collection of Open Mathematics Problems}, record 130 (GREEN-042). CC BY 4.0. \url{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}.
\item[S2] Ben Green, \emph{100 Open Problems}, maintained author collection, Problem 69 and its comments, accessed 7 October 2026. \url{https://people.maths.ox.ac.uk/greenbj/papers/open-problems.pdf}.
\end{description}
